Text engine check (textcheck)
=============================

Records how the text engine measures, lays out and draws a corpus of
strings, so that a change to the engine (HarfBuzz shaping, glyph
positioning, font fallback, ...) can be compared with the behaviour
before it: in "identity mode" widths and positions must stay bit for bit
the same, and any drawing difference must show up. It also checks a few
invariants. Like tests/eventloop it is not part of the normal build.

The corpus (textcheck.cpp, "corpus"): Latin (with kerning pairs, digits,
Romanian and Polish letters, combining marks, precomposed letters),
Greek (also with marks), Cyrillic, Arabic (plain, with harakat,
lam-alef), Hebrew (with and without points), bidi mixes (Arabic/Latin,
Hebrew/Latin, numbers in RTL text, LRM/RLM/RLE/PDF marks), Devanagari
(conjuncts), Thai, Chinese, Japanese, CJK mixed with Latin, Hangul
(syllables and conjoining jamo), emoji and mathematical letters (surrogate
pairs, a skin tone modifier, a regional indicator pair, VS16), symbols,
box drawing, tabs, control and format characters (soft hyphen, ZWSP,
ZWJ, ZWNJ, BOM), spaces only (NBSP, em space), the empty string, a
newline.

The fonts: Liberation Sans, Noto Sans, Liberation Serif, Liberation Mono
and Misc Fixed Wide (the only font with CJK on the machine the baseline
was made on: a bitmap font, 12x13ja/18px), each at 8, 9, 10, 12, 16 pt and
11, 13, 20 px, and Liberation Sans Bold and Liberation Serif Italic at
10 pt. Scripts the font lacks come from Qt 3's per-script fallback;
env.txt lists the font each script resolved to.

For each font and string, report.txt has (one line per measurement,
"<font> <string> <measurement> <value>", sorted):

  width, fm.prefix        QFontMetrics::width(s), width(s, len) for every
                          len
  fm.charWidth            charWidth(s, i) for every i
  fm.widthQChar           width(QChar) for every character
  fm.inFont               inFont(QChar) for every character
  fm.boundingRect         boundingRect(s)
  fm.boundingRectFlags    boundingRect(0, 0, 2000, 500, AlignLeft|AlignTop|
                          ExpandTabs, s) (what QLabel & co. use)
  fm.size                 size(ExpandTabs, s)
  layout.items            QLineEdit's QTextLayout (the same calls as
                          QLineEditPrivate::updateTextLayout()): per item
                          from+length@x,y, width, ascent, descent, rtl
  layout.widthUsed        QTextLayout::widthUsed()
  le.valid                QTextLayout cursor stops
  le.cursorToX            the cursor x of every valid position, as
                          QLineEditPrivate::cursorRect() computes it
  le.xToCursor            QLineEditPrivate::xToPos() of that x
  te.paragraphRect        QTextEdit (plain text, no wrapping, first
                          paragraph): QTextEdit::paragraphRect(0)
  te.charX, te.charWidth  the rich text engine's x and width of every
                          character (QTextStringChar::x, QTextString::width)
  te.valid                QTextString::validCursorPosition()
  te.cursorX              QTextCursor::x() after setCursorPosition(0, i)
  te.charAt               QTextEdit::charAt() at that x (a mouse click)
  draw.size, draw.ink,    QPainter::drawText(x, y, s) into a white pixmap:
  draw.pixels, draw.hash  its size, the box of the inked pixels relative
                          to the origin, their number and a hash of the
                          image (the PNG is in png/<font>/<string>.png)
  clusters.missing        start of the clusters (QTextLayout cursor stops,
  clusters.box            surrogate pairs kept whole) drawn one at a time
  clusters.blank          that have a character inFont() says is missing,
                          whose image contains the font's "missing glyph"
                          (tofu: the images of unassigned code points and
                          lone surrogates, "<font> - boxes"), or that
                          draw nothing although visible
  "<font> - metrics"      ascent, descent, height, leading, ...
  glyphs.le.n, glyphs.le.NN
                          the glyphs of QLineEdit's layout (above), from
                          its QTextEngine: the number of items, then one
                          line per item NN (logical order):
                            <from>+<length> s<script> l<bidi level>
                            [,space][,tab][,pos] font <engine>:<family>:<px>
                            | g <glyph ids> | a <advances> | o <x/y offsets>
                            | c <log clusters, one per character>
                            | t <glyph attributes>
                          (",pos": the shaper positioned the glyphs; the
                          attributes of a glyph: c cluster start, m mark,
                          z zero width, the combining class if not 0, "-"
                          for none)
  glyphs.draw.n, glyphs.draw.NN
                          the same for the items QPainter::drawText(x, y, s)
                          shapes (the same QTextEngine calls, on the text
                          as is: control characters are not replaced)

"-" in a list marks a position that is not a valid cursor position.

The glyph lines are written last for each string, so they cannot change
the other measurements; textcheck -noglyphs (run.sh -noglyphs) leaves
them out (the report is then the one of the harness before they were
added). They are what the HarfBuzz stages are checked with at glyph
level: the glyph ids, advances, offsets and log clusters of each item.

Checks (fails.txt has a FAIL line per failed check, summary.txt counts
them per script; NOTE lines are properties Qt 3 does not promise, kept
for information):

  width-stable       width(s) measured first and after everything else
                     agree (QFontMetrics caches widths per character, and
                     charWidth() puts contextual widths in that cache)
  width-len          width(s, len) == width(s)
  width-additive     width(s) == sum of charWidth(s, i)
  prefix-monotonic   width(s, k) <= width(s, k + 1)
  layout-width       QLineEdit's layout is as wide as QFontMetrics::width
  le-roundtrip,      x-to-cursor(cursor-to-x(i)) gives i or a position
  te-roundtrip       with the same x (*-roundtrip-exact, NOTE: gives i)
  le-monotonic       cursor x increases through LTR text and decreases
                     through RTL text (not checked for bidi text)
  le-surrogate-inside, te-surrogate-inside
                     no cursor stop between the halves of a surrogate pair
                     (*-surrogate-before, NOTE: a stop in front of a pair)
  te-adjacent        QTextEdit puts the characters of LTR text one after
                     the other (x[i + 1] == x[i] + width[i])
  te-width           QTextEdit's character widths add up to width(s)
  te-le-cursor       QTextEdit and QLineEdit put the cursor at the same x
                     (LTR text)
  ink-in-boundingRect  the drawn pixels are inside boundingRect(s)
  ink-nonempty       something is drawn for a string that is not blank
  ink-clipped        nothing is drawn at the border of the pixmap (else
                     textcheck's pixmap was too small)

Build against the library of this source tree, or of another tree with
QTDIR=... (textcheck uses QTextLayout, QTextEngine and the rich text
classes from the private headers, so build it against the tree whose
library it runs with; QTDIR must be a source tree, qfontengine_p.h needs
src/3rdparty/opentype and the Xft and FreeType headers, which build.sh
finds with pkg-config):

    tests/textcheck/build.sh                 # in tests/textcheck/build
    QTDIR=/other/qt tests/textcheck/build.sh tests/textcheck/build-other

Run (needs $DISPLAY; use a private X server with a fixed DPI, the results
depend on the DPI and on the server's Xft resources, see env.txt):

    Xephyr :86 -screen 1600x1200 -dpi 96 &
    DISPLAY=:86 tests/textcheck/run.sh [-b builddir] [-nopng] [-noglyphs] [label]

which writes tests/textcheck/results/<label>/ (default label: the short
commit of the tree, "-dirty" with uncommitted changes) and exits with 1
if a check failed. A run takes a few seconds. Compare two runs:

    tests/textcheck/compare.sh [-q] results/before results/after [diffdir]

which lists every measurement that differs (for lists, the indices that
differ), the FAIL/NOTE lines that are new or gone, and for each string
whose image differs the number of differing pixels, with a difference
image in diffdir if ImageMagick is installed. It exits with 0 only if
everything is identical. To check a change of the engine: build textcheck
for the tree before and after it, run both on the same X server, compare.

baseline/ has the report of the tree at the commit that added it (the
library in lib/ as built then), without the PNGs; it was run twice (in
two X server sessions) with identical results. The glyph lines were added
to it later (same library, same fonts), the other lines unchanged. It also depends on the
installed fonts, FreeType, fontconfig and Xft (env.txt), so after a
system upgrade compare against a fresh run of the old tree rather than
against baseline/.

Known failures at the baseline (bugs to know about, not fixed yet):

  le-roundtrip       Arabic, Hebrew, bidi, all fonts. RTL text: position 0
                     is at the right end of the line, and xToPos() returns
                     text.length() for an x at or after the right end, so
                     a click right of RTL text puts the cursor at the
                     logical end. Bidi text: at a run boundary the cursor
                     x of a position maps back to another position with a
                     different x.
  *-surrogate-inside every string with surrogate pairs: the cursor stop is
                     between the two halves of a pair instead of in front
                     of it, in QTextLayout and in QTextEdit (the cursor
                     can be put inside an emoji, not in front of it).
  layout-width       QLineEdit's layout and QFontMetrics::width() differ
                     for emoji (surrogates), conjoining jamo (width() adds
                     up the jamo one by one: 76 px instead of 28 at
                     Liberation Sans 10 pt), Hebrew with and without points
                     (1 px at 17 of 42 font sizes) and latin-comb in Misc
                     Fixed Wide at 12 and 16 pt.
  te-width, te-le-cursor
                     QTextEdit's widths of surrogate pairs (emoji,
                     math-alnum) differ from QFontMetrics' and QLineEdit's;
                     conjoining jamo as above; latin-comb in Misc Fixed
                     Wide at 12 and 16 pt.
  ink-in-boundingRect
                     boundingRect() misses drawn pixels (mostly 1-2 px) for
                     about half of the Arabic and Hebrew strings, some
                     Devanagari, emoji and Han, and a few Latin, Greek,
                     Cyrillic strings; the em space is drawn as a box in
                     Liberation Mono.

Coverage on the baseline machine (summary.txt, "clusters" lines): there
is no outline CJK font, Han and Hangul come from the bitmap Misc Fixed
Wide (the simplified Chinese U+4F60, U+6D4B, U+8BD5 are boxes in it,
and Qt 3 has no per-character fallback: one font per script); Arabic and
Thai fall back
to Microsoft Sans Serif with Liberation Sans; every half of a surrogate
pair is drawn as a box of its own (or nothing, with Liberation Serif);
U+035C (double breve below) alone draws nothing.

Other things found while writing this (not checked, because they are not
deterministic or need another setup):

  - QTextLayout::validCursorPosition(text length) reads the character
    attributes one past the end (random results; no caller in Qt uses
    it so, textcheck treats the end as valid without asking).
  - The patterns of the fallback fonts carry a point size computed with
    Qt's DPI, which Xft converts back with its own DPI (Xft.dpi or the
    server's): if the two differ (TEXTCHECK_DPI=96 on a 75 DPI server),
    the fallback fonts have the wrong size (10 px for a 13 px font).

Needs: g++, libX11, ImageMagick (optional, for the image comparison).
